Andrew Fabbro <andrew.fab...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> In the early game, I find a difficult balance between starving vs.
> fighting vs. healing.  It goes something like this:

That's a classic of early game survival skills.  Sounds like you are
doing fine.

> A. I need food, I'll go kill something and eat it.

Eat corpses early so you don't need food.  It is better to eat before
the Hungry flag comes on.  Eat until Satiated, don't eat until it
turns back off, eat again as soon as it's off.

Using pets has an interaction with this - Pets eat what you can
eat.  So I end up going in cycles of competing with my pet for
kills and food, then letting my pet take the lead while I'm Satiated.

> B. Whew, no longer hungry, but now I need to heal hit points.  I'll
> rest/try to avoid combat.

Something that can't be stressed enough - Missile combat kills
monsters before they can hurt you much or often before they can
hurt you at all.  Daggers, rocks, darts, you name it.  Even in the
early game you throwing missiles is more dangerous to monsters
than monsters shooting at you.  You gather missiles, they have
what they started with and numbers matter.  Armor matters, too.

Of course you're exploring while recovering.  Sometimes I like to
retreat up a level, hunt for slightly weaker monsters until mostly
recovered, then descend again.

> C. Uh-oh, hungry again, better go do some more killing, even though my
> hit points haven't quite healed up...

Note that the two early branches are also the ones stuffed to the
gills with food.  Sokoban has lots of food items.  The Gnomish
Mines have lots of edible corpses.  One thing I do early on in
the exploration of the mines is kill a monster, grab its corpse,
carry it upstairs, eat it, drop items near the stairs to be well away
from Burdened.  In time the number of monsters on the level drops
to the point I no longer feel I'm in a crowd of hostiles and I can
maneuver enough to recover safely on that level.  Doing this cycle
in the mines I often end up with enough missiles and armor that
I can do more combat, and I'm well fed.

Note that in the mines I'm careful to keep my pet from killing
and eating the majority of the monsters on a level.  So much
food that I didn't eat.  It's a trade-off between leading with the pet
while Satiated, competing with the pet while not, played closer
to the stairs while starting at each level.

> Lather, rinse, repeat, get a free comprehensive inventory
> identification.

Monsters id a lot of stuff by using it.

> So is this (a) something I'm not quite getting, or (b) the fundamental
> tension/challenge in the game.  Food really seems a lot bigger deal in
> NetHack than in the *bands.

Food strategy is an early one to build.  It is fundamental, but
only to the first several levels.  Learn it and the next skill comes
along ...

> I don't really have a problem finding food (I have only needed to pray
> for food once), but seem to always be on a food = fighting = lower hit
> points = trend towards death cycle.

Remote fighting with missiles to kill remotely, build up inventory
of armor to take less damage.  Melee is a last resort.

> Or is it the case that I'm not descending fast enough?

Controvertial topic.  Until about XP=5 I like to be at an XP level
when I descend to seriously explore a level.  By the time I return
from the mines that sort of waiting makes no sense at all though.

> I try to see most of a level before dropping down.

But descend a down stairs as soon as you find it.  Then climb
back up and continue exploring.  Initial monster strength will
be based on your XP at that point so leveling up later won't
matter until the level after that.

> Do things get easier once you
> get to levels that have more stores?

I don't think stores matter much for food.  The mines and
sokoban handle food.

> Or am I fundamentally missing
> something about how to heal up hit points?

Missile combat to reduce how many blows hit you to cause
damage combined with building armor to reduce how much
damage you get per blow and how many melee hits you take.
Daggers *rule*.

